			<title type="html"><![CDATA[Champagne and Bullets belongs on the Mount Rushmore of bad movies]]></title>
			<link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.theverge.com/column/971103/champagne-and-bullets-bad-movies-review" />
			<id>https://www.theverge.com/?p=971103</id>
			<updated>2026-07-26T17:24:07-04:00</updated>
			<published>2026-07-26T17:24:07-04:00</published>
			<category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Column" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Entertainment" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Film" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Movie Review" />
							<summary type="html"><![CDATA[There's something about a movie like The Room, Troll 2, or Fateful Findings that I find irresistible. These sorts of "so bad they're good" films are marvelous curiosities where ambition far outstrips resources, ability, and self-awareness to become something much greater than the sum of their parts. Champagne and Bullets (also released as GetEven and [&#8230;]]]></summary>
			
							<content type="html">
											<![CDATA[

						
<figure>

<img alt="Movie poster for Champagne and Bullets." data-caption="Thankfully the movie is bonkers enough to live up to the artwork. | Image: Vinegar Syndrome" data-portal-copyright="Image: Vinegar Syndrome" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/Champagne-and-Bullets.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100" />
	<figcaption>
	Thankfully the movie is bonkers enough to live up to the artwork. | Image: Vinegar Syndrome	</figcaption>
</figure>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There's something about a movie like <em>The Room</em>, <em>Troll 2</em>, or<em> Fateful Findings</em> that I find irresistible. These sorts of "so bad they're good" films are marvelous curiosities where ambition far outstrips resources, ability, and self-awareness to become something much greater than the sum of their parts. <em>Champagne and Bullets</em> (also released as <em>GetEven</em> and <em>Road to Revenge</em>) is a film on par with those classics of outsider cinema. </p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The movie is almost entirely the work of one man, John De Hart. Hart wrote, directed, and stars as Rick Bode in the film. He even performs a musical number early in the proceedings - The bizarre "<a href="https://youtu.be/zN2UuGfGr8k">Shimmy Slide</a>." During th …</p>
<p><a href="https://www.theverge.com/column/971103/champagne-and-bullets-bad-movies-review">Read the full story at The Verge.</a></p>
						]]>
									</content>
			
					</entry>
