			</author>
			
			<title type="html"><![CDATA[Marvel announces Ryan Gosling as Ghost Rider, a new Black Panther, and more]]></title>
			<link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/971071/marvel-ryan-gosling-ghost-rider-black-panther-comic-con" />
			<id>https://www.theverge.com/?p=971071</id>
			<updated>2026-07-26T10:51:33-04:00</updated>
			<published>2026-07-26T10:51:33-04:00</published>
			<category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Comic-Con" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Comics" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Entertainment" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Film" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Marvel" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="News" />
							<summary type="html"><![CDATA[While perhaps less dramatic than in years past, Marvel nonetheless had some big reveals lined up for Comic-Con. The highest profile was certainly the announcement that Ryan Gosling would be joining the MCU as Ghost Rider, with the Shawn Levy-directed (Deadpool &#38; Wolverine) standalone feature set to drop sometime in 2028. The studio also brought [&#8230;]]]></summary>
			
							<content type="html">
											<![CDATA[

						
<figure>

<img alt="Ryan Gosling on stage at Comic-Con being announced as Ghost Rider." data-caption="Ryan Gosling on stage at Comic-Con | Image: Marvel Studios" data-portal-copyright="Image: Marvel Studios" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/HOH7bVYboAAjHWn.jpeg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100" />
	<figcaption>
	Ryan Gosling on stage at Comic-Con | Image: Marvel Studios	</figcaption>
</figure>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">While perhaps less dramatic than in years past, Marvel nonetheless had some big reveals lined up for Comic-Con. The highest profile was certainly the announcement that Ryan Gosling would be joining the MCU as Ghost Rider, with the Shawn Levy-directed (<em>Deadpool &amp; Wolverine</em>) standalone feature set to drop sometime in 2028.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The studio also brought out Ryan Coogler, who officially confirmed <em>Black Panther III </em>is slated for release on December 15th, 2028. He also introduced David Jonsson (<em><a href="https://www.theverge.com/movie-reviews/775654/the-long-walk-review">The Long Walk</a>, <a href="https://www.theverge.com/2024/8/14/24219417/alien-romulus-review">Alien Romulus</a></em>) as T'Chala's son and the new Black Panther in the franchise.</p>
<div class="youtube-embed"><iframe title="Ryan Coogler introduces the new #BlackPanther, David Jonsson." src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/I6bzKjoKRAA?rel=0" allowfullscreen allow="accelerometer *; clipboard-write *; encrypted-media *; gyroscope *; picture-in-picture *; web-share *;"></iframe></div>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Marvel also showed off a new <em><a href="https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/967887/avengers-doomsday-trailer-marvel-release-date">Avengers: Doomsday</a></em> trailer and seemed t …</p>
<p><a href="https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/971071/marvel-ryan-gosling-ghost-rider-black-panther-comic-con">Read the full story at The Verge.</a></p>
						]]>
									</content>
			
					</entry>
			<entry>
			
			<author>
				<name>David Pierce</name>
			</author>
			
			<title type="html"><![CDATA[The vertical video takeover is here]]></title>
			<link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.theverge.com/column/970756/vertical-video-tiktok-youtube-instagram-streaming-facebook" />
			<id>https://www.theverge.com/?p=970756</id>
			<updated>2026-07-24T13:29:13-04:00</updated>
			<published>2026-07-26T08:00:00-04:00</published>
			<category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Column" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Creators" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Facebook" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Instagram" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Meta" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Social Media" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Streaming" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Tech" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="The Stepback" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="TikTok" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="YouTube" />
							<summary type="html"><![CDATA[This is The Stepback, a weekly newsletter breaking down one essential story from the tech world. For more on all things vertical video, follow David Pierce. The Stepback arrives in our subscribers' inboxes on Sunday at 8AM ET. Opt in for The Stepback here. How it started For a while, every social and media platform [&#8230;]]]></summary>
			
							<content type="html">
											<![CDATA[

						
<figure>

<img alt="" data-caption="" data-portal-copyright="Getty Images" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/gettyimages-2284214134.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100" />
	<figcaption>
		</figcaption>
</figure>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>This is </em><a href="https://www.theverge.com/the-stepback-newsletter">The Stepback</a><em>, a weekly newsletter breaking down one essential story from the tech world. For more on all things vertical video, follow </em><a href="https://www.theverge.com/authors/david-pierce"><em>David Pierce</em></a>. The Stepback<em> arrives in our subscribers' inboxes on Sunday at 8AM ET. Opt in for </em>The Stepback<em> <a href="https://www.theverge.com/newsletters">here</a>.</em></p>
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How it started</h2>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For a while, every social and media platform had its own identity. YouTube was for clips of TV shows and movies, and the home of so many members of a burgeoning creator community. Instagram was mostly pictures. Netflix was trying to be the on-demand HBO. Facebook was about friends. Twitter was about news. Snapchat was a messaging app.</p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">All these apps did have one important …</p>
<p><a href="https://www.theverge.com/column/970756/vertical-video-tiktok-youtube-instagram-streaming-facebook">Read the full story at The Verge.</a></p>
						]]>
									</content>
			
					</entry>
			<entry>
			
			<author>
				<name>Terrence O’Brien</name>
			</author>
			
			<title type="html"><![CDATA[Google basically confirms the Pixel 11 is getting a price hike]]></title>
			<link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/971041/google-confirms-pixel-11-price-hike" />
			<id>https://www.theverge.com/?p=971041</id>
			<updated>2026-07-25T14:13:36-04:00</updated>
			<published>2026-07-25T14:13:36-04:00</published>
			<category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Business" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Google" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Google Pixel" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="News" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Tech" />
							<summary type="html"><![CDATA[Google's Vice President of Devices and Services, Shakil Barkat, all but confirmed in an interview with 9to5 Google that its next Pixel phone would cost more than the Pixel 10. Considering the ongoing RAM supply issues due to the explosion of AI data centers, the rumored price hike is not a complete surprise. Companies from [&#8230;]]]></summary>
			
							<content type="html">
											<![CDATA[

						
<figure>

<img alt="A screenshot of the glowing orb on the camera bar of Google’s Pixel 11." data-caption="" data-portal-copyright="Image: Google" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/Screenshot-2026-07-15-at-11.36.30-AM.png?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100" />
	<figcaption>
		</figcaption>
</figure>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Google's Vice President of Devices and Services, Shakil Barkat, all but confirmed in an interview with <em><a href="https://9to5google.com/2026/07/24/google-pixel-11-price-increase/">9to5 Google</a></em> that its next Pixel phone would cost more than the Pixel 10. Considering the ongoing RAM supply issues due to the explosion of AI data centers, the <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/962145/the-pixel-11-could-come-with-a-higher-price-tag">rumored price hike</a> is not a complete surprise. Companies from <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/956903/apple-price-increase-2026-macbook-ipad-mac-home-vision-pro">Apple</a> to <a href="https://www.theverge.com/games/926606/nintendo-switch-2-price-hikes-earnings-results">Nintendo</a>, <a href="https://www.theverge.com/games/957042/xbox-price-increase-memory-shortage">Microsoft</a> to <a href="https://www.theverge.com/streaming/970814/roku-streaming-price-increase">Roku</a>, have all raised their prices in response to the soaring cost of memory. </p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Barkat said that Google had "shielded our consumers from supply fluctuations for as long as possible," but that the "economics have fundamentally shifted and we're not immune to that." He went on to tell 9to5 tha …</p>
<p><a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/971041/google-confirms-pixel-11-price-hike">Read the full story at The Verge.</a></p>
						]]>
									</content>
			
					</entry>
			<entry>
			
			<author>
				<name>Terrence O’Brien</name>
			</author>
			
			<title type="html"><![CDATA[Synth historian Oli Freke will spend big on a good bicycle]]></title>
			<link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/971013/oli-freke-bjooks-beat-gems-drum-machine-book-interview" />
			<id>https://www.theverge.com/?p=971013</id>
			<updated>2026-07-25T10:51:21-04:00</updated>
			<published>2026-07-25T11:15:00-04:00</published>
			<category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Books" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Entertainment" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Interview" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Report" />
							<summary type="html"><![CDATA[Oli Freke is a musician and journalist whose works have appeared in Sound on Sound, The Quietus, and Mixmag. This has included using math to explore the melodic potential of the Western 12-tone scale and deep dives on effects plug-ins. He's even written a book tracing the evolution of the synthesizer from 1963 through 1995, [&#8230;]]]></summary>
			
							<content type="html">
											<![CDATA[

						
<figure>

<img alt="" data-caption="" data-portal-copyright="" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/Oli-Freke-photo.jpeg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100" />
	<figcaption>
		</figcaption>
</figure>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Oli Freke is a musician and journalist whose works have appeared in <em>Sound on Sound</em>, <em>The Quietus</em>, and <em>Mixmag</em>. This has included using math to explore the <a href="https://plus.maths.org/how-many-melodies-are-there">melodic potential</a> of the Western 12-tone scale and deep dives on <a href="https://www.soundonsound.com/techniques/ableton-live-12-granulator-iii">effects plug-ins</a>. He's even written a book tracing the <a href="https://www.olifreke.co.uk/books">evolution of the synthesizer</a> from 1963 through 1995, charting the change from analog to digital and back. </p>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">His new project is <a href="https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/944102/beat-gems-is-a-big-beautiful-book-of-drum-machines"><em>Beat Gems</em></a>, a coffee-table visual history of drum machines from publisher <a href="https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/bjooks/beat-gems-drum-machines-in-modern-music/">Bjooks</a>. Like other Bjooks titles, <em>Beat Gems</em> is loaded with gorgeous photos, fascinating historical tidbits, and practical advice for making the most of your gear, whether it's  …</p>
<p><a href="https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/971013/oli-freke-bjooks-beat-gems-drum-machine-book-interview">Read the full story at The Verge.</a></p>
						]]>
									</content>
			
					</entry>
			<entry>
			
			<author>
				<name>Brad Bourque</name>
			</author>
			
			<title type="html"><![CDATA[Teenage Engineering’s unique music machines are 30 percent off]]></title>
			<link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.theverge.com/gadgets/970685/teenage-engineering-30-percent-deal-sale" />
			<id>https://www.theverge.com/?p=970685</id>
			<updated>2026-07-24T13:42:46-04:00</updated>
			<published>2026-07-25T11:00:00-04:00</published>
			<category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Deals" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Gadgets" /><category scheme="https://www.theverge.com" term="Verge Shopping" />
							<summary type="html"><![CDATA[With elements from synthesizers, loopers, and effects pedals, Teenage Engineering’s devices lie somewhere between musical instrument and modern art. These grooveboxes, as they’re often called, are capable of helping you produce songs in real time from a single unit, with an eye-catching design language. A wide swath of the brand’s offerings are 30 percent off [&#8230;]]]></summary>
			
							<content type="html">
											<![CDATA[

						
<figure>

<img alt="Close-up of OP-XY with four rows of buttons and several visible knobs." data-caption="Teenage Engineering’s OP-XY has a huge number of functions. | Photo by Terrence O’Brien / The Verge" data-portal-copyright="Photo by Terrence O’Brien / The Verge" data-has-syndication-rights="1" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/01/257534_Teenage_Engineering_OP-XY_TOBrien_0023.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100" />
	<figcaption>
	Teenage Engineering’s OP-XY has a huge number of functions. | Photo by Terrence O’Brien / The Verge	</figcaption>
</figure>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">With elements from synthesizers, loopers, and effects pedals, Teenage Engineering’s devices lie somewhere between musical instrument and modern art. These grooveboxes, as they’re often called, are capable of <strong>helping </strong>you produce<s> </s>songs in real time from a single unit, with an eye-catching design language. A wide swath of the brand’s offerings are 30 percent off at <a href="https://www.guitarcenter.com/Teenage-Engineering/">Guitar Center</a> when you use code <strong>TE30</strong>, at<strong> </strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/stores/teenageengineering/page/2DAAEEAE-215D-4176-BD1B-D9A11516FF25">Amazon</a> when you click the on-page coupon for whichever product you’re interested in, and B&amp;H Photo (discount applied automatically at checkout). The terms for the Amazon coupon claim it will run through August 8th, 2026, while Guitar Center deal is listed as “limited time.”</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <strong>OP-XY</strong> is one of the flagship units, which my colleague Terrence O&#8217;Brien described as a “greatest hits tour for Teenage Engineering.” Normally $2,299, the discount code brings the price down to a more palatable $1,609.30, which is a lot cheaper, but still pretty pricey for an aspiring musician. That said, it’s loaded with features for both production and live performance, and has a premium, durable build quality that reflects its price point. It’s currently sold out at Amazon, but in stock and discounted at <a href="https://www.guitarcenter.com/teenage-engineering/OP-XY-Portable-Synthesizer-1500000447897.gc">Guitar Center</a> and <a href="https://www.bhphotovideo.com/c/product/1856481-REG/teenage_engineering_te033as001_op_xy_dynamic_performance_sequencer.html">B&amp;H Photo</a>. <a href="https://www.theverge.com/reviews/608609/teenage-engineering-op-xy-review">Read our Teenage Engineering OP-XY review</a>. </p>
<div class="product-block"><h3>Teenage Engineering OP-XY</h3>
<figure class="product-image"><img width="300" height="200" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/01/257534_Teenage_Engineering_OP-XY_TOBrien_0005.jpg?w=300" class="attachment-medium size-medium" alt="" /></figure>
<h3>Where to Buy:</h3><ul><li><a href="https://www.bhphotovideo.com/c/product/1856481-REG/teenage_engineering_te033as001_op_xy_dynamic_performance_sequencer.html"> <strike>$2299</strike> $1609.3 at <strong>B&amp;H Photo</strong></a></li><li><a href="https://www.guitarcenter.com/teenage-engineering/OP-XY-Portable-Synthesizer-1500000447897.gc"> <strike>$2299</strike> $1609.3 at <strong>Guitar Center</strong></a></li></ul></div>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The model I’m most tempted by is the <strong>EP-40 Riddim</strong>, a compact sampler with a library of over 400 built-in synth settings and effects particularly well-suited for producing dub and reggae music, plus 128MB of storage for your own loops and recorded sounds. It’s a bit less intimidating for a beginner than the OP-XY, both in terms of price point and features, and its<strong> </strong>tighter genre focus may help concentrate your efforts and onboarding. Normally $329, the EP-40 is $230.30 at <a href="https://www.amazon.com/teenage-engineering-sampler-sequencer-handcrafted/dp/B0FXXQ88PV">Amazon</a>, <a href="https://www.guitarcenter.com/teenage-engineering/EP-40-riddim-Supertone-Original-Layering-Machine-1500000467630.gc">Guitar Center</a>, and <a href="https://www.bhphotovideo.com/c/product/1918016-REG/teenage_engineering_te032as006_ep_40_riddim_sampler_composer.html/">B&amp;H Photo</a> during the promotion.</p>
<div class="product-block"><h3>Teenage Engineering EP-40 Riddim</h3>
<figure class="product-image"><img width="300" height="300" src="https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/Teenage-Engineering-EP-40-Riddim.png?w=300" class="attachment-medium size-medium" alt="" /></figure>
<h3>Where to Buy:</h3><ul><li><a href="https://www.amazon.com/teenage-engineering-sampler-sequencer-handcrafted/dp/B0FXXQ88PV"> <strike>$329</strike> $230.3 at <strong>Amazon</strong></a></li><li><a href="https://www.guitarcenter.com/teenage-engineering/EP-40-riddim-Supertone-Original-Layering-Machine-1500000467630.gc"> <strike>$329</strike> $230.3 at <strong>Guitar Center</strong></a></li></ul></div>
						]]>
									</content>
			
					</entry>
